00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:53.990
<v Ethan>Well, first of all, in that night-- let's see-- just like there was such debauchery. It was ridiculous. We were like kids. We were playing spin the bottle, truth or dare. I was like, oh, no way! Holy cow, this is crazy.
<v Ethan>嗯，首先，那天晚上——让我看看——简直太堕落了。太荒唐了。我们就像孩子一样。我们在玩转瓶子，真心话大冒险。我当时想，噢，没门！天哪，这太疯狂了。

00:34:53.990 --> 00:35:10.470
<v Griffin Hansbury>But even Ethan encounters dating difficulties. He's always dated women casually, but it's different now that he's a guy. When he doesn't call someone for a week, or when he's busy with someone else on a Saturday night, he now gets angry emails from unhappy women. The old rules he used to play by have changed.
<v Griffin Hansbury>但即使是伊桑也遇到了约会困难。他一直随随便便地和女人约会，但他现在作为男人就不同了。当他一周没打电话给某人，或者周六晚上忙着和别人在一起时，他现在会收到不快乐女人的愤怒邮件。他以前玩的旧规则已经变了。

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:42.510
<v Ethan>Previously, it was sort of, there was-- you know, if I was dating a straight woman who is used to dating men, because I was a woman, I think she would feel safe. Like, she sort of felt like, well, there's safety here. I don't have to play games, because this is my equal. This is my friend. This is my partner. This is-- you know, feeling like we're on the same playing field. Everything's level. And I feel like that's definitely not the case, or not the approach that's taken when you're seen as a man.
<v Ethan>以前，这就好像是——你知道，如果我在和一个习惯和男人约会的直女约会，因为我是个女人，我觉得她会感到安全。就像，她有点觉得，嗯，这里有安全性。我不必玩游戏，因为这是我的平等对象。这是我的朋友。这是我的伴侣。这是——你知道，感觉我们在同一个赛道上。一切都是平等的。我觉得这绝对不是事实，也不是被视为男人时采取的方法。

00:35:42.510 --> 00:36:00.020
<v Griffin Hansbury>We've all had to adjust to these strange new codes of behavior. Before I looked like a man, I could talk to women about how I liked going to strip clubs, and that made me cool and edgy. Now it just makes me a jerk.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我们所有人都不得不适应这些奇怪的新行为准则。在我看起来像男人之前，我可以和女人谈论我喜欢去脱衣舞俱乐部，那让我看起来很酷很前卫。现在这只会让我看起来是个混蛋。

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:10.910
<v Griffin Hansbury>My friend Nate is a different story than the rest of us. Most of the guys in the group always wanted to be men and were attracted to women. Nate wanted to be a man, but he was attracted to other men.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我的朋友内特（Nate）和我们其他人不同。小组里的大多数男人一直想成为男人，并且被女人吸引。内特想成为男人，但他被其他男人吸引。

00:36:10.910 --> 00:36:24.210
<v Nate>One common response I get when I tell people I'm transgender is they say, well, why didn't you just stay a girl? If you're attracted to men, why not just be a straight woman? As if that's all it takes. It's just being attracted to men.
<v Nate>当我告诉人们我是跨性别者时，我得到的一个共同反应是，嗯，你为什么不直接当个女孩呢？如果你被男人吸引，为什么不直接当个直女呢？好像就这么简单。只是被男人吸引。

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:45.560
<v Nate>My entire persona. Like, my personality, everything about me is male. I mean, a straight woman, I may in fact be attracted to-- both be attracted to, say, Antonio Banderas, but I think that the ways in which we-- everything else is different. Who we are is different. Very different.
<v Nate>我整个人。就像，我的个性，关于我的一切都是男性的。我的意思是，直女，我可能实际上被吸引——我们都可能被安东尼奥·班德拉斯吸引，但我认为我们处理——其他一切都不同。我们是谁不同。非常不同。

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:57.230
<v Griffin Hansbury>It is a little hard to understand, I know. But today Nate is a cute gay man with close-cropped hair, stylish glasses, and a trim, compact build, looking for another cute gay man to date.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我知道这有点难理解。但今天内特是一个可爱的男同性恋，留着修剪整齐的短发，戴着时髦的眼镜，体型紧凑，正在寻找另一个可爱的男同性恋约会。

00:36:57.230 --> 00:37:15.450
<v Griffin Hansbury>In his search for love, Nate had a harder time than the rest of us figuring out his target audience. Like everyone else, he couldn't understand how a female-bodied person could be a gay man, and he went through a long process of elimination. He knew he wasn't a straight girl. He felt like a boy. Maybe he was a lesbian.
<v Griffin Hansbury>在寻找爱情的过程中，内特比我们其他人更难弄清楚他的目标受众。和其他人一样，他不明白一个女性身体的人怎么能成为男同性恋，他经历了一个漫长的排除过程。他知道他不是直女。他感觉自己像个男孩。也许他是拉拉。

00:37:15.450 --> 00:37:58.300
<v Nate>I made every possible effort. I bought all the folk music albums. I was good at the cultural stuff. I wore sandals. I went to Mexico and studied, you know, women and their struggles in Latin America. I own every Indigo Girls album ever made. Every Ani DiFranco album. Gosh. I became a vegetarian, had a heavily soy-based diet. I mean really! I'm not kidding. It was actually being a woman attracted to women that was the hard part.
<v Nate>我尽了最大努力。我买了所有的民谣专辑。我在文化方面表现不错。我穿凉鞋。我去了墨西哥学习，你知道，女性及其在拉丁美洲的挣扎。我拥有Indigo Girls发行过的每一张专辑。Ani DiFranco的每一张专辑。天哪。我成了素食者，饮食以大豆为主。我是说真的！我没开玩笑。其实作为一个被女人吸引的女人才是最难的部分。

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:08.800
<v Nate>I even went through a man-hating stage. Not that all lesbians did that. I mean, certainly, this was my attempt at being a lesbian. That came more from my resentment of everything men had that I wanted.
<v Nate>我甚至经历了一个讨厌男人的阶段。并不是说所有的拉拉都那样。我的意思是，当然，这是我试图成为拉拉的尝试。那更多来自我对男人所拥有的我想要的一切的怨恨。

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:44.920
<v Nate>I would just look at men on the subway and just feel so resentful. I felt like men just took for granted the fact that they looked like men. I'd look at these men just sitting there on the subway. I was like, God, if I were them, I would just be jumping up and down, jumping for joy. And they're just walking around, these schlumpy men, you know? Just all these different men, with their male chests and male genitalia and wearing a tie to work. Just not even appreciating it.
<v Nate>我当时只是看着地铁上的男人，感到非常怨恨。我觉得男人只是理所当然地认为他们看起来像男人。我看着这些坐在地铁上的男人。我当时想，上帝，如果我是他们，我会只是上下跳跃，欢呼雀跃。而他们只是在走来走去，这些邋遢的男人，你知道吗？只是所有这些不同的男人，有男人的胸部和男人的生殖器，系着领带上班。甚至一点都不珍惜。

00:38:44.920 --> 00:39:05.430
<v Griffin Hansbury>After the lesbian experiment failed, Nate finally realized what he had to do. He got chest surgery and started taking testosterone. Nate liked to joke that he's the only person he knows it's been L, G, B, and T-- the common shorthand for lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender. And for every letter, there's been a coming out to his parents. Think about that for a second.
<v Griffin Hansbury>拉拉实验失败后，内特终于意识到他该做什么。他做了胸部手术，开始服用睾丸素。内特喜欢开玩笑说他是他认识的唯一一个经历过L、G、B和T的人——拉拉、男同性恋、双性恋和跨性别的常用缩写。而且每一个字母，都有一个对他父母的“出柜”。想想这个。

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:17.790
<v Griffin Hansbury>But so far, all his hard work hasn't helped his love life much. Meeting men isn't too difficult for Nate. He's got a couple ads online, and gets regular responses. He goes out on a lot of first dates, but there aren't a lot of seconds.
<v Griffin Hansbury>但到目前为止，他所有的努力对他的爱情生活没什么帮助。结识男人对内特来说并不太难。他在网上有几个广告，并得到常规回复。他有很多次第一次约会，但没有多少第二次。

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:47.970
<v Griffin Hansbury>I haven't had anyone, you know, throw up like in The Crying Game. So I guess that's a good thing. But the best one was, I met this guy through a friend of a friend, and we met up, and walked around the city. And we ended up at Hudson River Park and we watched the fireworks at the end of the Gay Pride Parade. There's some beautiful fireworks at the end of the parade. And I don't know. I guess you could say it was romantic.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我还没遇到像《哭泣游戏》里那样呕吐的人。所以我猜这是件好事。但最好的一次是，我通过朋友的朋友认识了一个人，我们见面后，在城市里走来走去。最后我们在哈德逊河公园看了同性恋骄傲游行结束时的烟花。游行结束时有美丽的烟花。我不知道。我猜你可以说这很浪漫。

00:39:47.970 --> 00:40:15.270
<v Griffin Hansbury>We had a subsequent date, and at the very end of the date, I said, you know, there's something that's kind of hard to tell you, but I feel like it's time to let you know. And he said, what, you're transsexual? And he was joking. He was trying to come up with the weirdest thing that he could think of, just to make me feel better.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我们后来又约会了，在约会结束时，我说，你知道，有件事很难告诉你，但我觉得是时候让你知道了。他说，怎么，你是变性人？他是在开玩笑。他想试图说出他能想到的最奇怪的事情，只是为了让我感觉好一点。

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:37.890
<v Griffin Hansbury>And I wish I could just go out there and date whoever I wanted, and they didn't have to be this incredibly open-minded person, you know? My therapist the other day said, he actually said, we'd be both [BLEEP] ourselves if we didn't say that it's going to be incredibly difficult for you to find a life partner. And I'm thinking, God, I'm paying you for this?
<v Griffin Hansbury>我真希望我能随心所欲地出去约会任何人，他们不必是一个思想极其开放的人，你知道吗？我的心理医生前几天说，他实际上说，如果我们不说你找到生活伴侣会非常困难，那就是在自欺欺人。我在想，上帝，我付钱给你就是为了听这个？

00:40:37.890 --> 00:41:02.000
<v Griffin Hansbury>Still, Nate keeps trying. He's cute and funny, and he's playing the game, which is more than he ever did before transition, when he couldn't even get off the bench. I'm sure it's just a matter of time before Nate finds someone to curl up with on the couch to watch Will and Grace.
<v Griffin Hansbury>尽管如此，内特一直在尝试。他可爱又风趣，他在玩这个游戏，这比他在变性前他甚至不能离开板凳的时候做的多多了。我相信内特找到人一起蜷缩在沙发上看《威尔和格蕾丝》只是时间问题。

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:12.880
<v Griffin Hansbury>So you spend all this time waiting for love to come along, worrying that it never will, and wondering what you'll do with it now that you've got this new body. What happens then when love finally shows up?
<v Griffin Hansbury>所以你花所有的时间等待爱情降临，担心它永远不会来，并想知道既然你有了这个新身体，你会拿它做什么。当爱情终于出现时，会发生什么呢？

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:35.250
<v Griffin Hansbury>Ray, the Italian Stallion from earlier in my story, has been with his girlfriend Amy for nearly a year. She knew he was trans right from the start, because Ray's roommate introduced them. Ray fell for Amy the moment he first saw her, and she fell for him. But Amy had never dated a trans man and Ray had never dated a straight woman, so nobody made a move. Six months passed with no contact at all.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我故事里早先提到的“意大利种马”雷，已经和他的女朋友艾米（Amy）在一起快一年了。她从一开始就知道他是跨性别者，因为雷的室友介绍了他们。雷在第一次看到艾米时就爱上了她，她也爱上了他。但艾米从未和跨性别男性约会过，雷也从未和直女约会过，所以没人采取行动。六个月过去了，没有任何接触。

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:45.230
<v Griffin Hansbury>Finally, it was Amy who got things going. She dropped Ray an email and their courtship began at a glacial pace, including a long series of platonic lunches and dinners.
<v Griffin Hansbury>最后，是艾米开始了行动。她给雷发了一封电子邮件，他们的求爱以缓慢的速度开始了，包括一系列长时间的柏拉图式的午餐和晚餐。

00:41:45.230 --> 00:42:12.310
<v Amy>Well, it got to the point where it was ridiculous. Like, we went out to dinner, we heard this band, and we were sitting really close, sort of-- it was like a Moroccan restaurant-- we were sitting on pillows on the floor. And we were sitting there, and it was sort of like-- you were talking, but both of us were sort of like, kiss each other. In, I think, both of our minds, it was like, somebody has to kiss somebody. Sort of like you're talking, but then you lose track of what you're saying, because you can't even pay attention to it, because you're like, we've got to kiss, we've got to kiss. And we didn't.
<v Amy>好吧，这到了荒唐的地步。就像，我们去吃晚餐，听了这个乐队，我们坐得非常近，有点——这是一家摩洛哥餐厅——我们坐在地上的垫子上。我们坐在那里，就像——你在说话，但我们俩都觉得，亲吻彼此。我想，在我们两人的脑海里，都是——有人必须亲吻某人。就像你在说话，但随后你失去了你正在说的轨迹，因为你甚至无法注意它，因为你在想，我们必须亲吻，我们必须亲吻。但我们没吻。

00:42:12.310 --> 00:42:19.340
<v Griffin Hansbury>Finally, one night after a movie, Ray walked Amy home, and they lost themselves in conversation for 70 long blocks.
<v Griffin Hansbury>最后，看完电影的一个晚上，雷送艾米回家，他们在谈话中迷失了70个街区。

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:31.380
<v Ray>It's miles, yeah. It was definitely miles. And we weren't in walking shoes, and we were carrying bags, and it was nighttime, and there was a million different reasons why we should have just taken a cab or the subway, but we did not.
<v Ray>那是英里，是的。绝对是英里。我们没穿走路的鞋子，我们提着包，那是晚上，有几百万种理由为什么我们应该坐出租车或地铁，但我们没坐。

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:45.420
<v Ray>And she did say that she wasn't really sure, like, completely how she felt about the trans thing. And then at the same moment, or like the next moment, she'd be very physical with me. So I was getting these two different signals.
<v Ray>她确实说她不是很确定，比如，完全对跨性别这件事的感觉。然后就在那一刻，或者就像下一刻，她会对我很亲昵。所以我收到了这两个不同的信号。

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:56.070
<v Amy>When we took that 70-block walk and we ended up at my house, I thought, well maybe he'll kiss me goodnight. And then he kind of hugged me goodnight, and I was a little disappointed.
<v Amy>当我们走了那70个街区，最后到了我家时，我想，也许他会吻我晚安。然后他有点拥抱我晚安，我有点失望。

00:42:56.070 --> 00:43:11.830
<v Amy>But it was my choice. Like, I was the one who went, you know, saying, let's be friends. And then I don't know why I was disappointed that he didn't kiss me. I couldn't-- I was sort of confused-- and I couldn't really figure it out, what I was doing. What I was doing. I couldn't figure out what I was doing.
<v Amy>但这是我的选择。就像，我是那个说，你知道，让我们成为朋友的人。然后我不知道为什么我对没吻我感到失望。我不能——我有点困惑——我无法真正弄明白，我在做什么。我在做什么。我无法弄明白我在做什么。

00:43:11.830 --> 00:43:15.120
<v Griffin Hansbury>In retrospect, what do you think you were doing?
<v Griffin Hansbury>回顾过去，你认为你在做什么？

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:32.150
<v Amy>Because I liked him so much, I wanted to make sure that if we-- I just knew that every step I was taking was like a step I couldn't turn back.
<v Amy>因为我太喜欢他了，我想确保如果我们要——我知道我走的每一步都像是无法回头的一步。

00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:49.270
<v Griffin Hansbury>Romance is about overcoming obstacles. And in a way, Ray and Amy's story is the perfect plot for a romance novel. Girl meets boy. Girl grapples with the fact that boy used to be a girl. Girl falls in love with boy. I'm surprised Harlequin hasn't jumped on this one yet.
<v Griffin Hansbury>浪漫就是克服障碍。在某种程度上，雷和艾米的故事是浪漫小说的完美情节。女孩遇到男孩。女孩纠结于男孩曾经是女孩的事实。女孩爱上男孩。我很惊讶Harlequin还没用这个题材。

00:43:49.270 --> 00:44:08.300
<v Griffin Hansbury>I like to think that trans men give you the best of both worlds. I hear so many women say things like, I wish my gay best friend were straight for like a day. Well, that's us. We're your gay best friend, and we want to date you. We want to go shopping and take dance lessons with you. It's not a bad deal when you think about it.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我喜欢认为跨性别男性给你两全其美。我听到很多女性说这样的话，我希望我的男同闺蜜能直一天。好吧，那便是我们。我们是你的男同闺蜜，我们要和你约会。我们想和你一起购物和上舞蹈课。当你想到它时，这并不是一笔坏交易。

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:16.730
<v Griffin Hansbury>But of course, even with all the good stuff, no trans man is perfect. There is one big thing missing. Or maybe it's not so big.
<v Griffin Hansbury>但当然，即使有所有的好东西，没有跨性别男性是完美的。有一件大事缺失了。或者也许它并没有那么大。

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:29.310
<v Amy>So what everybody really wants to know is what our sex life is like. And it's weird, because when I talk about it, I feel like a science experiment.
<v Amy>所以每个人真正想知道的是我们的性生活是什么样的。这很奇怪，因为当我谈论它时，我觉得像个科学实验。

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:53.320
<v Griffin Hansbury>I mean, I get really sick of that question when people ask me, and here I am asking you about it. And as a trans man, I think that that's a question that-- an answer that is something that I really want to know, and that I'm afraid of. Like, do you miss having a penis? I think it's on every trans man's mind too.
<v Griffin Hansbury>我的意思是，当人们问我那个问题时，我感到很厌烦，而现在我正问你。作为跨性别男性，我认为那是一个问题——一个我真正想知道、但我又害怕的答案。就像，你怀念有阴茎吗？我想这也在每个跨性别男性的脑海里。

00:44:53.320 --> 00:45:40.660
<v Amy>It's a difficult question to answer. Because you love this person, and in some ways, it feels-- you know, modern technology has made it possible for it to be very similar. And you know, sometimes I think-- I don't necessarily say I would miss a penis. I missed an ease. I miss a little bit more simplicity. I miss the simplicity of it, I think. Not the actual body part.
<v Amy>这是一个很难回答的问题。因为你爱这个人，在某些方面，它感觉——你知道，现代技术已经使它可能变得非常相似。你知道，有时我想——我不一定说我会怀念阴茎。我怀念一种轻松。我怀念一点点简单。我想我怀念它的简单。而不是身体部位本身。

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:48.970
<v Griffin Hansbury>If you passed them on the street, you wouldn't know that Ray and Amy were anything more than ordinary. The only way you would know is if they told you.
<v Griffin Hansbury>如果你在街上经过他们，你不会知道雷和艾米除了平凡之外有什么特别。你唯一知道的方法就是他们告诉你。

00:45:48.970 --> 00:45:59.870
<v Griffin Hansbury>Coming out is something Amy never had to do until she fell in love with Ray. Now all of a sudden she's co-starring in The Crying Game, and people look at her differently too.
<v Griffin Hansbury>“出柜”是艾米在爱上雷之前从未做过的事情。现在突然间她成了《哭泣游戏》的联合主演，人们看待她的方式也不同了。

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:27.230
<v Amy>Half of your listeners, they're wondering what I'm like. They're wondering what I look like. Am I attractive enough? Couldn't I go to a bar in New York and pick out a nice single Jewish man? And I'm definitely the kind of girl who'd go to a bar. I look like every other cute girl New Yorker who wears expensive clothes and pointy-toed shoes. Like, I look like everybody else. And I'm choosing this. Like, I've chosen this person.
<v Amy>你的一半听众都在想我长什么样。她们在想我的样子。我够吸引人吗？我不能去纽约的一家酒吧挑个不错的单身犹太男人吗？我绝对是那种会去酒吧的女孩。我看起来像每个其他可爱的纽约女孩，穿昂贵的衣服和尖头鞋。就像，我看起来像其他人。而我选择了这个。就像，我选择了这个人。

00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:37.690
<v Amy>I fell in love with this amazing man. And all my friends who know him are jealous. Like they wish that they could meet a Ray.
<v Amy>我爱上了一个了不起的男人。所有认识他的朋友都很嫉妒。就像她们希望自己能遇见一个雷。

00:46:37.690 --> 00:47:08.360
<v Griffin Hansbury>Transition itself is in some ways the most romantic undertaking of all. It's an idealistic adventure, extravagant and kind of unreal. You commit yourself to a feeling early on, a dream you've had all your life, and then you do it, believing all the while that the impossible can be made possible, that the great divide can be crossed. It's a total leap of faith, like falling in love. You don't know what will happen when you get to the other side, but you go there anyway.
<v Griffin Hansbury>变性本身在某种程度上是最浪漫的事业。这是一种理想主义的冒险，铺张且有点不真实。你早早地把自己投入一种感觉中，一个你一生都有的梦想，然后你做了，始终相信不可能的事可以变得可能，那伟大的鸿沟可以跨越。这是一个完全的信念飞跃，就像坠入爱河。你不知道当你到达另一边时会发生什么，但你还是去了。

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:28.620
<v Ira Glass>Griffin Hansbury.
<v Ira Glass>格里芬·汉斯伯里。

00:47:28.620 --> 00:47:32.100
<v Ira Glass>Act Three. A Love Story.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕。一个爱情故事。

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:41.730
<v Ira Glass>Well, we wanted to end our show today with a love story. A little real-life fable with an answer to the question, what is this thing called love? Sarah Vowell believes that she has just the one.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我们今天想以一个爱情故事结束我们的节目。一个小小的现实生活中的寓言，回答了那个问题，这个叫爱情的东西是什么？萨拉·沃威尔相信她正好有这么一个故事。

00:47:41.730 --> 00:48:01.750
<v Sarah Vowell>The marriage of Johnny Cash and June Carter Cash is the greatest love story of the 20th century. The first time Johnny saw June, he was on his high school senior class trip to Nashville, and she was on stage at the Grand Ole Opry, singing with her famous relatives, the Carter family, and cracking jokes with Ernest Tubb.
<v Sarah Vowell>约翰尼·卡什（Johnny Cash）和琼·卡特·卡什（June Carter Cash）的婚姻是20世纪最伟大的爱情故事。约翰尼第一次见到琼时，他在高三班级旅行到纳什维尔，而她正在大奥普里剧院的舞台上，与她著名的亲戚卡特家族一起唱歌，并与欧内斯特·塔布（Ernest Tubb）开玩笑。

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:25.360
<v Sarah Vowell>Years later, in 1961, the Opry was where they would meet. They were backstage, and Johnny went up to June and told her, "You and I are going to get married someday." She laughed and told him that she couldn't wait. I'm guessing that when Cash went home that night, he didn't mention this to Vivian, his wife.
<v Sarah Vowell>多年后的1961年，奥普里正是他们相遇的地方。他们在后台，约翰尼走到琼面前告诉她，“你和我有一天会结婚的。”她笑了，告诉他她等不及了。我猜当卡什那天晚上回家时，他没向他的妻子薇薇安（Vivian）提起这件事。

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:58.620
<v Sarah Vowell>Not long after that, June joined Johnny's road show and they were traveling around the country together on tour. His wife mostly stayed home, and so did her husband. But June Carter was a lady-- a solid, accomplished, well-meaning Christian professional. And no solid, well-meaning Christian pro willingly falls in love with another woman's husband, especially if he's a pill-popping speed freak like Johnny Cash was back then.
<v Sarah Vowell>在那之后不久，琼加入了约翰尼的巡演剧团，他们一起在全国各地巡演。他的妻子大部分时间待在家里，她的丈夫也一样。但琼·卡特是一位女士——一位稳重、有成就、心地善良的基督徒职业者。而没有一个稳重、心地善良的基督徒职业者会心甘情愿地爱上别人的丈夫，尤其是如果他像当时的约翰尼·卡什那样是个吃药的药物瘾君子。

00:48:58.620 --> 00:49:22.530
<v Sarah Vowell>Years later, June would describe falling in love with Johnny this way. "I felt like I had fallen into a pit of fire and I was literally burning alive." So June, who had been a professional musician since she was as tall as a ukulele, called her songwriting partner, Merle Kilgore, and they wrote about June's forbidden feelings for Cash.
<v Sarah Vowell>多年后，琼会这样描述爱上约翰尼的感觉。“我觉得自己掉进了一个火坑，我真的正在活活燃烧。”所以琼，自她像尤克里里琴那么高起就是个职业音乐人，她给她的歌曲创作搭档梅尔·基尔戈尔（Merle Kilgore）打了电话，他们写了关于琼对卡什那禁忌感情的歌。

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:32.390
<v Sarah Vowell>It was a song straight out of Dante. They gave it to June's not particularly Dante-esque sister Anita, who recorded it as "Love's Ring of Fire."
<v Sarah Vowell>那是一首直接出自但丁的歌。他们把它给了琼不怎么但丁的妹妹安妮塔（Anita），她把它录制成了《爱的火环》（Love's Ring of Fire）。

00:49:32.390 --> 00:50:17.210
<v Anita Carter>[SINGING} Love is a burning thing and it makes a fiery ring. Bringing hurt to the heart's desire, I fell in the ring of fire. I fell into, into the burning ring of fire. I fell down, down, down, down, into the deepest mire. And it burns, burns, burns, burns, the ring of fire. The ring of fire. The ring of fire.
<v Anita Carter>[歌唱] 爱是一种燃烧的东西，它形成了一个火环。给心中的渴望带来痛苦，我掉进了火环。我掉进了，掉进了燃烧的火环。我掉下去，掉下去，掉下去，掉进最深的泥潭。它燃烧，燃烧，燃烧，燃烧，那个火环。那个火环。那个火环。

00:50:17.210 --> 00:50:44.170
<v Sarah Vowell>Johnny Cash heard Anita Carter's record, and then one night, he had a dream. He dreamed of mariachi horns playing this song. Some say it was the influence of the then-popular Tijuana Brass. Others say it was the barbiturates he took before bed to take the edge off the amphetamines. Either way, Johnny cash went into the studio with a couple of trumpet players and made his dream come true.
<v Sarah Vowell>约翰尼·卡什听了安妮塔·卡特的唱片，然后有一天晚上，他做了一个梦。他梦见墨西哥流浪乐队（mariachi horns）吹奏这首歌。有人说这是当时流行的蒂华纳铜管乐队的影响。其他人说这是他在睡前为了减轻安非他命影响而服用的巴比妥类药物。无论如何，约翰尼·卡什带着几位小号手进了录音室，让他的梦想成真了。

00:50:44.170 --> 00:51:04.580
<v Johnny Cash>[SINGING] Love is a burning thing, and it makes a fiery ring.
<v Johnny Cash>[歌唱] 爱是一种燃烧的东西，它形成了一个火环。

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:37.030
<v Sarah Vowell>The peculiarity of the song's orchestration is topped only by the weirdness of its credits. Here was a married man singing a song written about him by the betrothed woman who was in love with him, and singing the record back-up "oohs" were said woman, June Carter, as well as her sister and her mother. Mother Maybelle Carter, the most respectable elder in the history of country music, if not America itself. Listen to how cheerful they sound.
<v Sarah Vowell>这首歌编曲的奇特性只有它名单的怪异程度能与之媲美。这里有一个已婚男人唱着由他心爱且爱着他的未婚女人为他写的歌，而为这张唱片进行“噢”伴唱的正是那位女人，琼·卡特，以及她的妹妹和母亲。梅贝尔·卡特母亲（Mother Maybelle Carter），乡村音乐史上、甚至美国历史上最受尊敬的长辈。听听她们听起来有多快乐。

00:51:37.030 --> 00:51:53.940
<v Johnny Cash And The Carter Family>[SINGING] I fell into a burning ring of fire. I went down, down, down, and the flames went higher. And it burns, burns burns, the ring of fire, the ring of fire.
<v Johnny Cash And The Carter Family>[歌唱] 我掉进了一个燃烧的火环。我掉下去，掉下去，火焰烧得更高。它燃烧，燃烧，燃烧，那个火环，那个火环。

00:51:53.940 --> 00:52:15.910
<v Sarah Vowell>In this song, to compare love to fire, isn't just the pop music sexy heat cliche like "you give me fever" or "hunka, hunka burning love" or "it's getting hot in here." This is fire as in brimstone, old-time religion, written by the daughter of a people who believed in the eternal flames of hell.
<v Sarah Vowell>在这首歌里，将爱比作火，不仅仅是像“你给了我发烧”或者“嗯嗯，燃烧的爱”或者“这里变得热起来了”这种流行音乐里的性感热度陈词滥调。这是硫磺般的火，古老的宗教，由一个相信地狱永恒火焰的人的女儿写的。

00:52:15.910 --> 00:52:32.460
<v Sarah Vowell>June Carter was coveting her neighbor's spouse, which meant she was breaking one of the Ten Commandments. Loving Johnny Cash was a sin, and for her, the wages of sin were death-- a death in which the sinner spent all eternity as nothing more than kindling.
<v Sarah Vowell>琼·卡特在觊觎邻居的配偶，这意味着她在违背十诫之一。爱上约翰尼·卡什是种罪过，而对她来说，罪的代价就是死亡——一种罪人永恒的时间里只剩下柴火般的死亡。

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:53.990
<v Sarah Vowell>When June Carter admitted to herself that she loved Johnny Cash, it is, in a small Country and Western love song way, not unlike the moment Huck Finn resolves to help the slave Jim escape, even though he's been told that doing so would be wrong. "All right then," he says. "I'll go to hell."
<v Sarah Vowell>当琼·卡特向自己承认她爱约翰尼·卡什时，这在某种小型乡村音乐爱情歌的方式中，与哈克·费恩（Huck Finn）决定帮助奴隶吉姆（Jim）逃跑的时刻没有区别，尽管他被告知这样做是不对的。“那好吧，”他说，“那我就去地狱吧。”

00:52:53.990 --> 00:53:08.150
<v Sarah Vowell>This is from June's recording of "Ring of Fire." Notice, no trumpets. Just an old-timey fiddle, and she strums her autoharp, maybe ribbing her husband a little by pointing out in the liner notes, "That's the way I've heard it from the beginning."
<v Sarah Vowell>这是来自琼录制的《火环》。注意，没有小号。只有一把老式的琴，她弹奏着她的自奏琴，也许通过在内页说明中指出“这就是我从一开始听到的方式”来稍微调侃一下她的丈夫。

00:53:08.150 --> 00:53:27.210
<v June Carter Cash>[SINGING] The taste of love is sweet when hearts like ours meet. I fell for you like a child. Oh, but the fire went wild.
<v June Carter Cash>[歌唱] 当像我们这样的心相遇时，爱的滋味是甜的。我像个孩子一样为你沦陷。哦，但火势变狂了。

00:53:27.210 --> 00:54:01.970
<v Sarah Vowell>There's an acre of misgiving in that little word, "oh." As in, oh, what have I done. Oh, his poor wife. Oh, Lord, forgive me. Oh, for crying out loud, I better flush his pills down the toilet yet again. Oh, I am Grand Ole Opry royalty, and this belligerent drug addict is about to get banned from my beloved Opry for smashing out the stage lights with a mike stand, and oh, I'm going to love him anyway.
<v Sarah Vowell>在那句小小的“哦”字里，有一英亩的懊悔。就像，哦，我做了什么。哦，他可怜的妻子。哦，主啊，原谅我。哦，大声哭出来，我最好再把他的药冲进马桶里。哦，我是大奥普里剧院的女王，而这个好战的毒瘾者即将因为用麦克风架砸碎舞台灯光而被我心爱的奥普里封杀，哦，我还是要爱他。

00:54:01.970 --> 00:54:21.300
<v Sarah Vowell>"Ring of Fire" became a number one hit for Johnny Cash in 1963. Then, finally, he got divorced, and June got divorced, and he got sober, and in 1968 they got married.
<v Sarah Vowell>《火环》在1963年成为约翰尼·卡什的一首冠军单曲。然后，最终，他离婚了，琼也离婚了，他戒了毒，在1968年他们结婚了。

00:54:21.300 --> 00:54:45.770
<v Sarah Vowell>When I say that Johnny Cash and June Carter Cash's romance was the greatest love story of the 20th century, I'm not just thinking about their fiery courtship, though your better love stories do require that obstacles are overcome, and the Cashs had plenty of those. I was really thinking about their marriage-- 35 years of actual happily ever after.
<v Sarah Vowell>当我说约翰尼·卡什和琼·卡特·卡什的浪漫是20世纪最伟大的爱情故事时，我不仅是在思考他们炽热的求爱，尽管你们更好的爱情故事确实需要克服障碍，而卡什夫妇有很多那种障碍。我其实是在想他们的婚姻——35年真正的从此幸福快乐。

00:54:45.770 --> 00:55:21.720
<v Sarah Vowell>The pictures of the two of them together through the years are almost suspiciously entertaining to look at. Johnny's cracking June up, or she's cracking him up, or in my favorite, the photo on his compilation album, Love , she's fallen asleep against his shoulder. His chin is resting on her head, and he might be smelling her hair. In his liner notes next to that picture, Johnny Cash says that he's sitting there thinking about Robert Browning's poem about the death of his wife Elizabeth Barrett Browning, when June Carter Cash walks in and wonders what he'd like to eat for lunch.
<v Sarah Vowell>多年来他们俩在一起的照片看起来几乎是令人怀疑的有趣。约翰尼让琼大笑，或者她让他大笑，或者在我最喜欢的，他精选集《爱》（Love）的封面上，她靠在他的肩膀上睡着了。他的下巴抵在她的头上，他可能在闻她的头发。在那张照片旁边的内页说明里，约翰尼·卡什说他正坐在那里思考罗伯特·勃朗宁关于他妻子伊丽莎白·巴雷特·勃朗宁去世的诗，这时琼·卡特·卡什走进来，想知道他午饭想吃什么。

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:36.270
<v Sarah Vowell>And that seems about right-- the two of them in a room together, pondering sandwiches and poetry. Grandly, he often credited her with saving his life. But he also bragged, she likes the same kind of movies I do.
<v Sarah Vowell>这看起来差不多——他们俩在房间里，考虑着三明治和诗歌。宏大地，他经常把拯救他生命归功于她。但他也吹嘘，她喜欢和我看一样的电影。

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:58.630
<v Sarah Vowell>On June's solo album, Press On , after her "Ring of Fire," the very next song is a duet with Johnny. It's a gospel ballad narrated by an old married couple worried that one will die before the other. Worried about getting left behind. "If it proves to be as well that I am first to cross," June sings, "and somehow I have a feeling it will be."
<v Sarah Vowell>在琼的个人专辑《坚持》（Press On）中，在她的《火环》之后，紧接着的一首歌是与约翰尼的二重唱。这是一首福音民谣，由一对老夫妻叙述，担心一个会比另一个先死。担心被抛在后面。“如果事实证明我先跨越是神的旨意，”琼唱道，“不知怎么的，我有种感觉会是这样。”

00:55:58.630 --> 00:56:26.010
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>[SINGING] But if it proves to be his will that I am first to cross, and somehow I've a feeling it will be. When it comes your time to travel, likewise don't feel lost. For I will be the first one that you'll see.
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>[歌唱] 但如果事实证明这是神的旨意，我先跨越，不知怎么的，我有种感觉会是这样。当你旅行的时间到了，同样不要感到迷失。因为我会是你看到的第一个人。

00:56:26.010 --> 00:56:56.350
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>And I'll be waiting on the far side banks of Jordan. I'll be sitting, drawing pictures in the sand. And when I see you coming, I will rise up with a shout and come running through the shallow water, reaching for your hand.
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>我将在约旦河的远岸等待。我会坐着，在沙地上画画。当我看到你走过来，我会带着欢呼起身，跑过浅水，向你伸出手。

00:56:56.350 --> 00:57:11.360
<v Sarah Vowell>As she predicted in the song, June died first. Four months later, Johnny was buried next to her, because, as he once said, "This thing between us has been happening since 1961, and I just don't want to travel if she can't come with me."
<v Sarah Vowell>正如她在歌中所预测的那样，琼先去世了。四个月后，约翰尼被埋在她旁边，因为正如他曾经说过的，“我们之间这事从1961年就开始了，如果她不能和我一起，我只是不想旅行。”

00:57:11.360 --> 00:57:26.260
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>[SINGING] So I'll just rest here on the shore and turn my eyes away until you come and we'll see paradise."
<v June Carter And Johnny Cash>[歌唱] 所以我会在这里休息，转过头去，直到你走过来，我们就会看到天堂。”

00:57:26.260 --> 00:57:30.740
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell has got a brand new book, Assassination Vacation, that comes out in April.
<v Ira Glass>萨拉·沃威尔有一本全新的书，《暗杀假期》（Assassination Vacation），四月份出版。

00:57:30.740 --> 00:57:44.570
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. Our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself with Diane Cook, Wendy Dorr, and Starlee Kine. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Stacy Tiderington.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。我们的节目今天由亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格（Alex Blumberg）和我自己制作，还有黛安·库克（Diane Cook）、温迪·多尔（Wendy Dorr）和斯塔莉·基恩（Starlee Kine）。我们的高级制片人是朱莉·斯奈德（Julie Snyder）。伊丽莎白·迈斯特（Elizabeth Meister）负责我们的网站。 Todd Bachmann和Stacy Tiderington提供制作帮助。

00:57:44.570 --> 00:57:55.730
